---
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import SessionsGrid from "../../components/SessionsGrid.astro";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={sessionLabels.plural}
  description={`The twelve-week teaching schedule: what happens in each ${sessionLabels.singular.toLowerCase()}, what to prepare, and what leaves the room.`}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p class="lede">
    Lectures are Mondays and seminars are Thursdays, which gives you three days
    with the reading and the argument before you have to say anything about
    either. The seminar is where the course actually happens; the lecture exists
    to put the evidence in front of you so that the seminar can do something
    with it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each week below lists what you should be able to do by the end of it. Those
    are written so that you can tell whether you have met them without asking us
    — if you cannot state the thing in your own words, you have not met it yet,
    and the seminar is the cheapest place to find that out.
  </p>

  <p>
    Regarding preparation: there is reading every week, and it is primary
    literature rather than a textbook. The papers are listed on each week's page.
    Most weeks one of them is the paper the seminar turns on, and the seminars
    where nobody has read it are visibly worse, which is the only enforcement
    mechanism we have and in practice it is enough.
  </p>

  <p>
    Weeks 8 to 10 look like a change of subject and are not. They are the
    control group — lineages that solved the same problems on different
    hardware — and they are there to test whether the account built in weeks 1
    to 7 was an account of intelligence or just an account of us.
  </p>

  <SessionsGrid />
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--at-border);
    padding-inline-start: 1.1rem;
    margin-block-end: 2rem;
  }
</style>
